# Recursive proof versus compressed replay benchmark

## Intent and scope

The user asks for representative histories, comparison against compressed replay
including usable snapshots, and actual proof-generation timing. The existing
[[experiments/incremental-state-proof/RECURSION-RESULTS]] measured tiny numeric
messages without ordinary compression. That comparison can exaggerate useful
savings and hide proving cost. This benchmark changes no circuit, key, native
language, production API or specification status. It measures the current model.
Tier 2 evidence/benchmark harness around a Tier 1 experimental proof system;
cryptographic soundness and production review remain open as previously recorded.

Only UInt32 sequential per-signer registers are proved by this model. Use clearly
labelled deterministic simulations: existing four-writer monotone control, a
many-writer status board with repeated small states, and many-writer telemetry
with changing bounded readings. These are representative patterns within the
supported language, not production traces or proof coverage of chat/document
payloads. A workload preference was requested asynchronously; use the supported
patterns if no alternative is supplied. Do not pad messages or attach unproved
payloads to improve a claimed compression ratio.

## Comparison and snapshot contracts

Replay encoding is one compact JSON array of canonical native message strings.
Compare raw bytes, gzip level 9 and Brotli quality 5 using built-in Node zlib;
measure compression/decompression time and verify byte-exact round trips. Apply
identical compression choices to each complete checkpoint bundle, rather than
adding separately compressed sizes or choosing only the favorable codec.
Message signatures are not present in these synthetic fixtures and not counted.

A current-view snapshot contains the complete sorted per-signer head list
(signer index, native address, sequence, numeric value) plus the sibling hashes
for the next empty history append slot. Its declared JSON shape is
`{"version":1,"heads":[{"signer":u32,"address":"sha256-<hex64>","seq":u32,"value":u32}],"appendSiblings":[fieldString,...]}`.
Heads have distinct strictly increasing signer indexes below 65536; addresses
are lower-case hex; field strings are canonical decimal integers below the field
order; the sibling array has exactly 16 entries. Reconstruct the head-tree root
and authenticate the next-append path at the proven count before using values.
Reject malformed/unknown fields, duplicate signers, missing or tampered heads,
wrong object, and wrong append siblings. This includes metadata needed to
validate the displayed current values and a first append witness. It does not
claim standalone long-term availability of all future witnesses.

A history-retaining snapshot contains ordered compact operation records
`{"version":1,"records":[{"signer":u32,"seq":u32,"value":u32,"address":"sha256-<hex64>","replaces":null|address}]}`.
Its root and count reconstruction must equal the selected checkpoint, and the
rendered historical entries must equal native `all`. This mode retains enough
model data for the native historical projection; it is not constant-sized.
The compact tuple encoding itself is structural compression and must not be
credited to cryptography. Both snapshot forms retain the existing host boundary
for native byte/address/authentication mapping.

Report proof + snapshot bundles, with the separately pinned key excluded for a
warm client and included for a first-download byte accounting. A downloaded key
still requires independent trust. Do not call proof-only bytes a usable state
snapshot. The full historical view and current view are distinct client needs.

## Execution and acceptance

- B1: measure byte-exact raw/gzip/Brotli replay and snapshot encodings at 128,
  256, 1024 and 4096 writes for each simulation. Bound native oracle input to its
  existing 4096-record/1 MiB grammar. Larger unproved rows are comparison baselines,
  never presented as new proof measurements.
- B2: actually generate a complete 256-write monotone proof by extending the
  existing verified 128-write recursive proof. Validate key/object and prefix
  roots against the generated data first. This is still a whole-history proof,
  not a newly trusted prefix. Report extension wall time separately; derive
  cumulative genesis-to-256 proving time only from the recorded actual prefix
  and extension calls, explicitly labelled as measured across runs.
- B3: generate complete telemetry and status histories from a timed fresh empty
  base, targeting 128 telemetry writes and 32 status writes. Compile once and
  reuse the same key and empty-base proof. Separate setup, genesis, witness
  construction, proof-call totals, verification, and per-case end-to-end time.
  Shared genesis/setup costs must be stated, not silently charged or omitted.
- B4: reconstruct both snapshots against the measured proof's actual roots and
  native state, and show negative tests reject corruptions. Produce each saved
  proof+snapshot bundle and verify its serialized proof against the expected
  object/tip. Measure actual bundles at proved lengths. At larger lengths, use
  clearly marked fixed-size-proof projections only if shown; the primary report
  must keep them separate from measured bundles.
- B5: save machine/runtime/settings, timing and size data, scripts, source hashes
  and a fresh-context adversarial benchmark review. Do not claim lossless message
  recovery from the proof, general CBCL equivalence, improved native authentication,
  or production performance guarantees.

## Resource envelope and exit

Branch `experiment/recursive-proof-benchmark`. Keep previous experiment evidence
unchanged; write a new `evidence/benchmark/` tree. One proof worker, 4 GiB sampled
process-tree RSS, 1 GiB disk reserve, 60-minute managed benchmark run and 65-minute
total active proof-run timebox. Record stops as partial with the exact completed
lengths; never pass a missing-case row. Use incremental progress records. No
production writes, participant messages, dependency changes or private datasets.
The maintainer acts next on the measured tradeoff and decides whether a more
complete kernel or a different proof backend merits a successor specification.

Producer: Codex; system model family GPT-6, exact version not exposed. Session
settings. Intent: “yes, and measure the time to generate the profo”. Inputs:
current recursive experiment, native oracle and fixture corpus. Design sequence:
separate represented data from proof; define current versus history-retaining
snapshots; use symmetric standard compression; preserve actual whole-history
coverage and distinguish resumed from fresh proving time. Reviews and results
are recorded after execution.
